A||N||5||vettepaul||Fender Alignment?||||1||
Z||000000||vettepaul||01-11-2001||06:12 PM||||Hi Guys,  I have an '86 GT over here in the U.K.It has what I think is called the aero package also.My problem is that one of the front fenders is a little higher than the door.As far as I am aware the car has had no accident damage.Is there a way of slightly lowering the fender to line up better with the door? Any answers would be much apreciated!....Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||01-11-2001||07:39 PM||||It's more likely either the hinges are worn or the hinges are out of adjustment. read the door & window service articles in my cave. <A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Fiero Cave</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tigger||01-11-2001||09:53 PM||||If your hinges are good, the next possibility could be that the hinges or whole door had been replaced.  I've seen this before, and I don't know if it's caused by the mill and drill process used on the attachment "pads" on the subframe.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||01-12-2001||09:50 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by vettepaul:<BR><B>Hi Guys,  I have an '86 GT over here in the U.K.It has what I think is called the aero package also.My problem is that one of the front fenders is a little higher than the door.As far as I am aware the car has had no accident damage.Is there a way of slightly lowering the fender to line up better with the door? Any answers would be much apreciated!....Thanks.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>if those hinges are ok, check to see if there are washer/shims along the inside of the top of fender where the mounting bolts are. if there are removing them will lower the fender the thickness of washers, adding will raise the fender. you can adjust the door up slightly too by loosening the bolts in the hinges and lifting it slightly and retighten.---it dont take much---an 1/8 of an inch can make a huge differnce in how door fits.good idea to mark with a pencil where the hinge is before u loosen it so you can go back if you mess up.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||vettepaul||01-12-2001||01:36 PM||||Thanks Guys,   By what you are saying I think the problem may well be in the door.It may be coincidence but since I had my new wheels put on a few days ago the door has been catching on the front fender as it is opened,could the wheel shop have caused this by where they jacked it up? By the way rogergarrison,I had a look under the fender bolts and there were NO washers.Between you all I think everything points towards the door.....                      Thank you all,  Paul.         ||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||01-12-2001||07:58 PM||||Tigger, Vette, The doors can be moved from one car to another. The problem is few people really understand all the posible adjustments you can make to a car door. The adjustments are very particular. All the things Vettepaul is discribing are typical signs of a door with bad hinges or hinges that have not been setup right.<P>WARNING! Door adjustments WILL ALWAYS affect the window! Window adjustment must always be checked anytime the door is adjusted. you can damage the door seal and window hardware. in extream cases you could break the window. There are articles under construction in me cave about this. <A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Fiero Cave</A>||||reg||1
